#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119536 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119950 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce50e834-b8c1-56da-8750-241601cd1046" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Recursive Formula for a Geometric Sequence"}] :question/id #uuid "0a5038b8-1c2e-591c-9190-9493014fd98a" :question/math-academy-id "e-1506" :question/problem "What is the recursive formula for the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n1, \\: 4, \\: 16, \\: \\ldots\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula for a geometric sequence with common ratio $r$ takes the form\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = r \\cdot a_{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the first term is $a_1={\\color{blue}1},$ and we can calculate the common ratio $r$ as\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{a_2}{a_1} = \\dfrac 4 1 = {\\color{red}4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula is\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = {\\color{red}4} \\cdot a_{n} , \\qquad a_1 = {\\color{blue}1}, \\qquad n\\geq 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119537 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119951 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aee1b1d-3cb6-5f52-8973-13b067b1c41a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Term of a Geometric Sequence Given Its Recursive Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "25a92b77-314f-5e6a-8a26-5e631d10e4d0" :question/math-academy-id "e-1507" :question/problem "What is the $4$th term of the geometric sequence given below?\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = -3 a_n,\\qquad a_1 = 2,\\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the recursive formula to compute the terms of the sequence up to the $4$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =2 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =-3{a}_{1}=-3(2)=-6 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =-3{a}_{2}=-3(-6)=18 \\\\ {a}_{4} & =-3{a}_{3}=-3(18)=-54\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a_4 = -54.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119538 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119953 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76437951-0d71-548b-b330-e494aa12c6a1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Geometric Sequences"}] :question/id #uuid "33621cf4-4508-51d5-958b-18a6ec1ebc73" :question/math-academy-id "e-1508" :question/problem "Which of the three sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $10, \\: 20, \\: 30, \\: 40, \\: \\dots$\n\nII. $a_{n+1} = \\dfrac{1}{6}a_n,\\quad a_1 = 36,\\quad n\\geq 1$\n\nIII. $f(n+1) = 2f(n)-1,\\quad f(1) =2,\\quad  n\\geq 1$" :question/worked-solution "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{20}{10} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{30}{20} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is a geometric sequence. The recursive rule $a_{n+1} = \\dfrac{1}{6}a_n$ tells us that each term is $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ times the previous term, so the common ratio is $r=\\dfrac{1}{6}.$\n\n- Sequence III is not a geometric sequence. To see this, we can calculate the first $3$ terms of the sequence and then compute the ratios of consecutive terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =2 \\\\ f(2) & =2\\cdot f(1)-1=2\\cdot 2-1=3 \\\\ f(3) & =2\\cdot f(2)-1=2\\cdot 3-1=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first two pairs of consecutive terms have different ratios:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(2)}{f(1)}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{f(3)}{f(2)}=\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only sequence II is a geometric sequence."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119584 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119952 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aae4cf92-f456-5b5f-bf92-1c4aed5ee4f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Term of a Geometric Sequence Given a Recursive Formula in Function Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "ae9a3613-9e84-53bd-ad35-48394adfdc30" :question/math-academy-id "e-4232" :question/problem "What is the $5$th term of the geometric sequence given below?\n\n$$\nf(n+1) = -5f(n),\\quad f(1) = -1,\\quad  n\\geq 1\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the recursive formula to compute the terms of the sequence up to the $4$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-1 \\\\ f(2) & =-5f(1)=-5(-1)=5 \\\\ f(3) & =-5f(2)=-5(5)=-25 \\\\ f(4) & =-5f(3)=-5(-25)=125 \\\\ f(5) & =-5f(4)=-5(125)=-625\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(5)=-625.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120171 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119951 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aee1b1d-3cb6-5f52-8973-13b067b1c41a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Term of a Geometric Sequence Given Its Recursive Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "801cf6ff-9c43-59b4-97d9-599eaa1e94e6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117192}] :question/id #uuid "385a6fb7-8690-582d-88d4-8765c8c27e63" :question/math-academy-id "q-249805" :question/problem "Consider the following geometric sequence:\n$a_{n + 1} = \\frac{1}{4}a_{n}, a_{1} = 256, n \\ge 1$The $4$th term of this geometric sequence is $a_{4} =$\n{{field-1}}."}]}
